        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "If someone wants to take cremated remains (“ashes”) from Canada to India—especially to immerse them in the entity[\"river\",\"Ganges River\",\"sacred river in India\"] as part of Hindu rituals—there are both airline rules and Indian entry requirements to follow. It’s very doable, but you need proper documentation.\n\n### 1. What you’re allowed to carry on a plane\nAirlines generally **allow cremated remains in carry-on baggage** (not checked baggage, for safety and respect).  \n- The urn/container must be **non-metallic and scannable** (wood, plastic, biodegradable urns are best).  \n- Security (like entity[\"organization\",\"Canadian Air Transport Security Authority\",\"airport security Canada\"]) must be able to X-ray it. If they can’t scan it, they may refuse it.\n\n👉 Always check your airline’s policy (e.g., entity[\"company\",\"Air Canada\",\"Canadian airline\"] or entity[\"company\",\"Air India\",\"Indian airline\"]), but rules are broadly similar.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Documents you will need\nCarry **originals (and copies)** of:\n- Death certificate  \n- Cremation certificate  \n- Letter from funeral home confirming contents  \n- Your ID/passport  \n- (Optional but helpful) notarized affidavit explaining purpose (religious immersion)\n\nThese documents may be checked at departure, transit, or arrival.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Entering India with ashes\nIndia generally permits cremated remains, but:\n- Declare them if asked at customs  \n- Keep documents handy  \n- No special “permit” is usually required for ashes alone, unlike full human remains\n\nIf you want certainty, you can contact the nearest Indian consulate (e.g., entity[\"organization\",\"Consulate General of India Toronto\",\"diplomatic mission Canada\"]) before travel.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Transport inside India & immersion\nOnce in India:\n- You can travel domestically with the urn  \n- Many people go to cities like entity[\"city\",\"Varanasi\",\"city on Ganges River India\"], entity[\"city\",\"Haridwar\",\"Ganges pilgrimage city India\"], or entity[\"city\",\"Rishikesh\",\"spiritual town India\"]  \n- Local priests (pandits) can guide the ritual (called *asthi visarjan*)\n\n---\n\n### 5. Practical tips\n- Use a **sealed, clearly labeled urn**  \n- Keep everything in your **carry-on bag**  \n- Arrive early at the airport (extra screening time)  \n- Avoid metal urns (they often cause issues with scanning)\n\n---\n\n### 6. If you don’t want to travel personally\nSome families use specialized services that arrange immersion ceremonies in India remotely, but many prefer to go in person for religious reasons.\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can walk you through a **step-by-step checklist from Toronto airport to Varanasi**, including which airlines are easiest and how to handle customs smoothly."
          ]
        },
