        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "An **urn** is simply a container used to hold cremated remains (ashes). It can be made from different materials depending on purpose, tradition, and travel needs.\n\n### Common materials used for urns\n\n**1. Wood**\n- Natural and traditional look  \n- Easy for airport X-rays (good for flying)  \n- Often used for temporary or permanent storage  \n\n**2. Metal (brass, aluminum, stainless steel)**\n- Durable and decorative  \n- Common in many cultures  \n- ⚠️ Not ideal for air travel because security scanners may not see inside clearly  \n\n**3. Ceramic / Clay**\n- Handcrafted and traditional  \n- Sometimes used in Hindu rituals  \n- Can be fragile  \n\n**4. Biodegradable materials**\n- Made from paper, salt, sand, or plant fibers  \n- Designed to dissolve in water (very suitable for immersion in the entity[\"river\",\"Ganges River\",\"sacred river in India\"])  \n- Common choice for Hindu rites  \n\n**5. Stone / Marble**\n- Heavy and long-lasting  \n- Usually for permanent placement, not travel  \n\n**6. Glass**\n- Artistic and decorative  \n- Fragile, not ideal for transport  \n\n---\n\n### Best choice for your situation (air travel + Ganges immersion)\nIf your goal is to carry ashes from Canada to India and immerse them:\n- Use a **non-metal urn** (wood or biodegradable)  \n- Many families use a **temporary plastic or cardboard container** for travel, then transfer ashes to a biodegradable urn in India  \n\n---\n\n### Important note\nThe ashes are usually sealed inside a **plastic bag within the urn**, so the outer container is mainly for protection and ritual.\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can suggest **exact urn types you can buy in Toronto** that are airline-safe and appropriate for Hindu immersion rituals."
          ]
        },
